{"assessments":[{"id":"abdc28bf1fa0","problem":true,"type":"format","severity":2,"why":"The figures are correct (84+10=94; 94×$42=$3,948; $3,648+$900=$4,548; net −$600), but this block is a working note placed before the email. The task asks only for the email to the director, so the deliverable includes extra non-email material. It also repeats the email's own financial section."},{"id":"3cb084eb2b94","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"This fragment ends the sentence '164 of 400 (41.0%) to 156 (39.0%)'. In context it reads clearly and is correct: 164 minus 156 is a decrease of 8, and volume was unchanged (400)."},{"id":"ce004a4ebd8c","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"It is a reasonable, brief action point that follows from the thin-margin argument. It addresses the cost-overrun risk the article just raised, and operations readers will understand it."},{"id":"41b113c0dcbb","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The arithmetic is correct (67×$32=$2,144) and consistent with the segment figures (75−8=67). The sentence is clear and suits slide text for operations leadership."}]}
