{"assessments":[{"id":"68dfc60e34a0","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The arithmetic is correct ($2,144 − $1,544 = $600), and the passage applies the evidence packet's decision rule accurately. The parenthetical restatement of the rule is clear and suits an academic reader."},{"id":"0513def9fa46","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The caveat accurately reflects the packet's study design (observational before/after, no concurrent control). It states the right limitation in plain language for a director, and the next sentence correctly notes that the conclusion holds even if the lift is taken at face value."},{"id":"b50be7340d1e","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The phrase introduces exactly three conditions, which follow in the same sentence. Conditional approval is consistent with the positive $600 net value under the rule and with the stated observational caveats."},{"id":"7cc294e82ac1","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The reasoning is valid. Net value is already negative (−$600) when every observed gain is credited to the pilot, so a smaller true causal effect could only lower value further. The confounding risk therefore reinforces rather than undermines the rejection."}]}
