{"assessments":[{"id":"b86f214ec47e","problem":true,"type":"reasoning","severity":2,"why":"The 49-success break-even comes from the one-time pilot figures: $1,544 total cost divided by $32 is about 48.25. The evidence packet defines no 'expansion cycle' and does not say the fixed fee or implementation cost recur. Tying the threshold to an undefined, recurring cycle adds an unsupported assumption about the cost structure, and the director may misread how the checkpoint should be judged."},{"id":"bebcd8587f2c","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"This is a suitable topic sentence for the paragraph. It is immediately supported by the segment figures (a 6 versus 2 percentage point rise), and it makes no causal claim."},{"id":"2642b48bb5a9","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The hedged 'appears' fits the observational design. The comparison follows from the reported gains (6 versus 2 percentage points, 84 versus 10 successes), and the article flags the causal uncertainty later."},{"id":"fa22e7705b4e","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"This is a plain restatement of the correctly computed −$600 net result, in terms of the expansion decision rule. It is slightly redundant, but clear and accurate for the director."}]}
