{"assessments":[{"id":"499bb9db3e68","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The headline matches the body. A net gain of 67 successes yields $2,144 against $1,544 in costs, a positive $600 that meets the decision rule. 'Modest' and 'Despite Study Limitations' accurately signal the small margin and the observational design."},{"id":"985664f4937d","problem":true,"type":"reasoning","severity":3,"why":"The passage claims the design 'isolates the intervention's impact' because volume was held constant. Equal segment volumes do not isolate causal impact in an uncontrolled before/after comparison. Seasonality and other time effects remain confounded. Slide 4 itself admits this, so the claim overstates the evidence and contradicts the deck's own caveat."},{"id":"f57c117bc9e4","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The logic is sound. If observed value is an optimistic upper bound and net value is already negative (−$600), the true net is likely even more negative. The design weakness therefore reinforces rejection rather than undermining it."},{"id":"f6e0d2aebabe","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The segment-limited recommendation is supported by the deck's own figures. Standard alone adds 75 successes ($2,400), which covers the $1,544 total cost, while complex declined. It is a defensible refinement of the expand decision, and the caveats appear later on Slide 4."}]}
