{"assessments":[{"id":"1037ec7a33c9","problem":true,"type":"reasoning","severity":2,"why":"'Clearly works' overstates causal certainty. The evidence is an uncontrolled observational before/after comparison, and the article's own point 2 says the gains could reflect seasonal effects or other confounds. That makes the claim internally inconsistent and unsupported."},{"id":"a2c8b13e54e5","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The claim is reasonable for this reader. Binomial variation on these segment sizes gives a period-to-period SD of roughly 25–30 successes for the before/after difference, so a 19-success swing plausibly falls within noise. It is stated as a judgment that supports the fragility point, not as a packet fact."},{"id":"91fc7727ad50","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"The phrase is immediately made concrete ('ideally a randomized or staggered rollout across segments') and directly addresses the observational-design caveat. It is appropriate wording for an academic research audience."},{"id":"76b8cdcae71f","problem":false,"type":"none","severity":0,"why":"This is a clear framing sentence for web readers. It previews the walkthrough, and the article supports the 'thin margin' claim with the 28% / 19-success analysis. 'Headline' plausibly refers to the positive top-line result."}]}
